From: DolphinSlicer <build@localhost>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 04:32:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] detect_bridging_direction: platform-independent order of
 candidate directions

Candidate bridge directions are collected in a std::unordered_map<double, Vec2d> and the
first minimal-cost one in iteration order is chosen. Costs often tie (opposite and
perpendicular edges of a square overhang), and unordered_map iteration order depends on
the standard library, so Linux, macOS and Windows chose different bridge directions.

Add PortableHashOrder.hpp, which reproduces the iteration order of libstdc++'s
unordered_map<double, T> (hash, prime rehash policy and node linking; verified against
libstdc++ 15 on 12000 randomized key sets), and iterate the candidates in that order.
Linux results are unchanged; other platforms now match Linux.
Upstreamable: yes, though a simpler deterministic tie-break may be preferred upstream.
